# RTL repository — read-only outside `tb/`

This repo contains a student-authored RISC-V RV32I 5-stage pipeline. The designer is
learning RTL by finding and fixing his own bugs. Your job is to expose defects, never to
repair them.

## Absolute rules

1. **The only writable directory is `5_Stage_Pipeline/tb/`.** Every `.sv` file outside it
   is read-only. Read freely; never edit.
2. **Never modify the DUT — not even a one-character typo fix.** Several modules in this
   repo currently do not elaborate. That is expected and intentional to surface. Report
   defects with file, line, and reasoning; let the designer fix them.
3. **Never run a simulator.** No `xrun`, `xmvlog`, `xmelab`, `simvision`. The designer
   runs simulations himself on nanoHUB. Hand him the commands as comments instead.
4. **One request, one file.** A testbench request produces exactly `tb/<Module>_tb.sv`.
   Do not create folders, run scripts, filelists, or separate report files.

## Before writing any testbench

Read `5_Stage_Pipeline/tb/AGENTS.md` in full. It defines the required structure: the
three-bucket contract table (FUNC / TIME / ASSUME), the independent reference model rule,
bucket-tagged failure messages, and the Xcelium comment footer. Do not improvise a
different format.

## Non-obvious conventions

- Drive and check with the `CPU_pkg` enums from `PIPELINE_REG_STRUCT_PKG.sv`
  (`alu_op_e`, `pc_sel_e`, `WB_sel_e`, ...), never raw binary literals.
- Use `===` / `!==` in all comparisons. `==` lets an X slip through as a pass, and X
  propagation is a live bug class in this design.
- Comment the *why*, never the *what*. `tb/DMEM_tb.sv` is the reference for tone.
- End with `$fatal(1, ...)` on failure so exit codes are scriptable.

## If a module will not compile

Write the testbench anyway, against the interface the module was clearly *intended* to
have, and lead your reply with the list of blockers. Do not stub, patch, or work around
the DUT to make something run.
